AB  - Many people are now concerned because Machine Learning (ML) that its applications have been growing rapidly in health, finance and national security. As learning systems store a lot of user information now, it is important to protect user privacy without damaging the way ML algorithms learn. Privacy Preserving Machine Learning (PPML) is able to solve this problem by including privacy protections within the learning algorithms. To make sure computation and data are secure, cryptography is one of the most important techniques today. In this paper, we focus on the use of new cryptographic tools in PPML, looking at their main ideas, frameworks for applying them and how well they perform compared to others. We analyze how Homomorphic Encryption (HE), Secure Multiparty Computation (SMPC), Differential Privacy (DP) and Functional Encryption can be used in centralized and federated ML configurations. We reviewed how effective each technique is, how it performs under high load, and what kinds of security risks exist – making it easier to decide if they can be used in practice.
