AB  - The exponential growth of Large Language Models (LLMs) has catalyzed breakthroughs in natural language processing, yet their deployment in sensitive domains such as healthcare, finance, and education raises significant privacy concerns. The classic fine-tuning on the centralized model exposes user data to a risk of leakage, and even the federated methods are vulnerable to gradient inversion attacks. The paper suggests a new model of privacy-sensitive fine-tuning of LLaMs in the form of homomorphic aggregation of encrypted gradients. Our vision is to create a secure federated network with LLaMA 7B as the foundation architecture, where each client node computes the gradient on private data and encrypts them with the CKKS scheme, allowing to train in a secure end-to-end fashion without exposing sensitive data. A homomorphic operation is performed to aggregate the encrypted gradients at a central server which is re-distributed allowing the collaborative fine-tuning of the model across parties that do not trust each other. The results of the experiments done on the benchmark datasets (AG News and SST-2) indicate that the proposed method has a 90.33 % accuracy, which is just a 0.93 % lower than centralized fine-tuning (91.26) and provides a high level of privacy protection. Gradient sparsification and quantization were used to optimize communication overhead and encryption latency to ensure a total per-round overhead of less than 1.7 seconds. Besides, system proved to be resistant to gradient leakage and membership inference attacks, with less than 0.01 probability of leakage.
ER  - 

