AB  - The rise of cloud computing has revolutionized data storage and computation paradigms, enabling organizations to leverage scalable infrastructure. However, data confidentiality remains a critical concern when outsourcing computation to third-party cloud providers. Homomorphic Encryption (HE) allows computation to be performed on encrypted data without decryption, providing a mathematically sound approach to privacy preservation. This paper presents an extended performance evaluation and optimization analysis of HE techniques compared to conventional encryption schemes such as AES and RSA in representative cloud environments. We simulate three real-world use cases — Electronic Health Records (EHR), Financial Data Processing, and Machine Learning Model Evaluation — to assess the computational overhead, latency, and memory requirements. Further, we propose a hybrid cryptographic architecture that combines partial HE operations with symmetric encryption to balance security, efficiency, and scalability. The study ends with recommendations for improvement. These include batching ciphertext, using parallel computation, and applying compression strategies.
